diff --git a/settings/legacy_settings.go b/settings/legacy_settings.go
index a50172e8c..2562f153a 100644
--- a/settings/legacy_settings.go
+++ b/settings/legacy_settings.go
@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@ type LegacySettings struct {
 	BlockFailureBackoffBase          time.Duration `key:"legacy_blockFailureBackoffBase" desc:"Base wait before re-attempting a block after a transient storage/service failure" default:"5s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Linear backoff unit, multiplied by the consecutive failure count and capped at legacy_blockFailureBackoffMaxDuration" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhen processing a legacy-sync block fails with a transient storage/service error (e.g. Aerospike overload surfacing as a client timeout), the same block is re-delivered and would otherwise immediately re-run the full multi-million-record decorate at full concurrency, re-loading an already struggling node. This sets the base per-block backoff: re-deliveries of a failed block hash are cheaply skipped until the backoff window elapses.\n\n### How It Works\nBackoff grows linearly with the consecutive failure count (base, 2xbase, 3xbase, ...) and is capped at legacy_blockFailureBackoffMaxDuration. The skip is non-blocking — the block handler returns a retryable error rather than sleeping, so the single block-processing goroutine is never stalled.\n\n### Values\n- **Default 5s** - quick first retry, backs off further on repeated failure\n- **0** - disables the per-block backoff (re-attempt immediately, prior behaviour)\n\n### Related\nComplements the Aerospike server-side overload retry (#1085), which handles DEVICE_OVERLOAD/MAX_ERROR_RATE but deliberately not client TIMEOUT. This block-level backoff (#1187) sits one layer up and throttles whole-block re-decorate storms."`
 	BlockFailureBackoffMaxDuration   time.Duration `key:"legacy_blockFailureBackoffMaxDuration" desc:"Cap on the per-block backoff window and the failure-tracking map TTL" default:"150s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Also bounds how long a failed block hash is remembered before its failure count resets" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nCaps the linear per-block backoff window (see legacy_blockFailureBackoffBase). It bounds only the retry SPACING; the failure-tracking map TTL is deliberately longer (this cap plus legacy_peerProcessingTimeout), so the consecutive-failure count survives one full slow failing attempt and the linear ramp actually engages on the overload path (a single failing outpoint decorate can outlast this cap, and a TTL equal to the cap would expire the entry mid-attempt and reset the count to its base every time).\n\n### Values\n- **Default 150s** - bounded retry cadence for a persistently failing block; long enough that a transient overload clears between attempts, and deliberately kept below the 180s sync-peer stall window (maxLastBlockTime) so a backed-off block reliably clears its window before the stall detector would rotate the delivering peer\n- Should be >= legacy_blockFailureBackoffBase to be meaningful\n- Keep below the 180s stall window; a larger value risks a rotated-in peer re-delivering a still-backed-off block\n- **0 (or any value <= 0)** - disables the per-block backoff entirely (the tracking map is not created)\n\n### Note\nWhen enabled, memory is bounded two ways: entries self-evict after the map TTL (this cap + legacy_peerProcessingTimeout), and the tracking map has a hard size cap, so only currently-failing block hashes are held."`
 	BlockPrefetchBufferBytes         int64         `key:"legacy_blockPrefetchBufferBytes" desc:"Memory budget (bytes) for blocks downloaded ahead of processing during legacy sync. 0 disables prefetch (synchronous ingestion)." default:"268435456" category:"Legacy" usage:"Bounds the total serialized bytes of received-but-not-yet-processed blocks across all legacy peers" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDecouples the per-peer network read-loop from per-block processing during legacy/IBD sync so block download overlaps validation, while bounding the memory held by blocks that have been received but not yet processed.\n\n### How It Works\nThe read-loop normally blocks until each block finishes processing, so download never overlaps processing (the next block's ~seconds-long download is serialized after the previous block's validation). With a non-zero budget the read-loop instead admits a block against a global byte-weighted semaphore and continues reading the next block while the previous one is validated in the background. The budget is the hard ceiling on the total serialized size of in-flight (received, queued, or mid-validation) blocks across ALL legacy peers and streams. A single block larger than the whole budget is admitted alone (it waits for everything else to drain first), preserving the original one-block-at-a-time backpressure for huge blocks.\n\n### Options\n- **0** - Disable prefetch; fall back to the original synchronous ingestion (one block in flight, full TCP backpressure). Use as a kill switch.\n- **>0** - Enable bounded async prefetch with this byte budget.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Larger budget** - More download/processing overlap and higher small-block throughput, more resident memory for buffered blocks.\n- **Smaller budget** - Less memory, less overlap; at/below one block's size it degrades to synchronous behaviour.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 256 MiB hides the inter-block download gap in the small-block era while keeping buffered-block memory well bounded.\n- The weight is a block's serialized size; the live decoded object graph is a small multiple, so size the budget below the headroom you actually have.\n- Big-block era: a block at least as large as the whole budget is admitted alone (its weight is clamped to the budget), giving zero download/validation overlap — identical to pre-prefetch behaviour. To get overlap on large blocks, raise the budget to at least ~2x the typical block size so more than one is in flight.\n- Set to 0 to revert to proven synchronous behaviour if a regression is suspected."`
+	AdvertiseFullNode                bool          `key:"legacy_advertiseFullNode" desc:"Advertise NODE_NETWORK to legacy peers regardless of block persister state" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable on private networks whose peers only sync from full nodes" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nForces the legacy service to advertise itself as a full node (NODE_NETWORK) instead of deriving the service bits from the block persister height.\n\n### How It Works\nBy default the legacy service advertises NODE_NETWORK only when the block persister is running and within the retention window of the best block; otherwise it advertises NODE_NETWORK_LIMITED (pruned). The decision is made once, when the service starts. SV Node syncs only from NODE_NETWORK peers, so a fresh regtest node (nothing persisted yet) is never used as a sync source by SV Node peers. With this setting enabled the service advertises NODE_NETWORK from the start; block bodies are served from the blockchain, subtree and UTXO stores as usual.\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Always advertise NODE_NETWORK\n- **false** - Derive from the block persister state (default)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Enable on private/regtest networks where SV Node peers must follow this node\n- Keep disabled on public networks unless the node really keeps full block history"`
 	Upnp                             bool          `key:"legacy_upnp" desc:"Enable UPnP for automatic port mapping" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable automatic port forwarding via UPnP" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables Universal Plug and Play (UPnP) for automatic port forwarding on compatible routers.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled, the node will:\n- Attempt to discover UPnP-capable routers on the local network\n- Automatically configure port forwarding for incoming connections\n- Periodically renew the port mapping lease\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Enable UPnP port mapping\n- **false** - Disable UPnP (manual port forwarding required)\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Easier setup, requires UPnP support on router\n- **Disabled** - More secure, requires manual router configuration\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep disabled in production for security\n- Enable for home/development setups behind NAT\n- Ensure router supports UPnP if enabled"`
 	PeerRegistryEnabled              bool          `key:"legacy_peerRegistryEnabled" desc:"Mirror connected legacy peers into the centralized peer registry" default:"true" category:"Legacy" usage:"Makes legacy peers visible in the dashboard alongside libp2p peers" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nMirrors every connected legacy (Bitcoin wire protocol) peer into the centralized peer registry hosted by the blockchain service, so the dashboard can show legacy peers alongside libp2p peers.\n\n### How It Works\nA reconcile goroutine in the legacy service periodically snapshots its connected peers and registers each one with transport type TRANSPORT_WIRE_PROTOCOL. Entries carry wire-protocol-only fields: direction, protocol version, service flags, ping, clock offset, starting height, sync-peer flag and wire connect time.\n\nThis is a read-only visibility path. It feeds no sync, catchup or peer-selection decision.\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Register legacy peers (default)\n- **false** - Kill switch; the reconcile loop does not start\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep enabled for operator visibility\n- Disable only if the extra registry traffic is unwelcome on a node with very many legacy connections"`
 	PeerRegistrySyncInterval         time.Duration `key:"legacy_peerRegistrySyncInterval" desc:"How often legacy peers are reconciled into the centralized peer registry" default:"10s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Lower values refresh the dashboard sooner and cost more registry traffic" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls how often the legacy service pushes its connected-peer snapshot into the centralized peer registry.\n\n### How It Works\nEach tick snapshots the connected legacy peers and pushes only what changed. An unchanged peer costs no RPC. A peer that disappeared is marked disconnected once, then left for the registry TTL to reap.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Shorter interval** - A connect or disconnect appears sooner in the dashboard, more registry RPCs\n- **Longer interval** - Less traffic, staler dashboard\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 10 seconds is well below the legacy 2-minute ping interval\n- Values under 1 second are pointless: the underlying peer statistics do not change that fast"`
